{:edb/db-before-t 556 :edb/db-after-t 557 :edb/tx-data [[13194139533869 50 #inst "2026-10-05T06:00:45.937Z" 13194139533869 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262444 13194139533869 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262451 13194139533869 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262458 13194139533869 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262500 13194139533869 true] [17592186083227 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262507 13194139533869 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262465 13194139533869 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262472 13194139533869 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262486 13194139533869 true] [17592186083228 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262493 13194139533869 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262479 13194139533869 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262514 13194139533869 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262521 13194139533869 true] [17592186083229 1020 #edb/ref 17592186262528 13194139533869 true] [17592186083870 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533869 true] [17592186083870 1145 "Let $A$ be the event that the chosen component was produced by machine $A$, and let $D$ be the event that the chosen component is defective. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the component was produced by machine $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected component is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(D\\cap A),$ we take the number of defective components produced by machine $A,$ and divide by the total number of defective components $(37+43=80).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap A)=\\frac{5}{80}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(D\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of defective components produced by machine $B,$ and divide by the total number of defective components $(37+43=80).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(D\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{3}{80}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected component is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D\\cap A)+P(D\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{80}+\\frac{3}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{80} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{10}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186083873 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533869 true] [17592186083873 1145 "Let $G$ be the event that the chosen student obtained the top grade, and let $A$ be the event that the student is from Group $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the student is from Group $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected student obtained the top grade, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(G)=P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $35%$ of her students are in Group $A$ and $65%$ are in Group $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.35,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.65.\n$$\n\n- Since $20%$ of the students in Group $A$ obtained the top grade, we have\n\n$$\nP(G|A)=0.20.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected student from either group obtained the top  grade is $0.265,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(G)=0.265.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(G|{A}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(G) & =P(G|A)P(A)+P(G|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.265 & =(0.20)(0.35)+P(G|{A}^{′})(0.65) \\\\ 0.265 & =0.07+0.65P(G|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.65P(G|{A}^{′}) & =0.195 \\\\ P(G|{A}^{′}) & =0.30\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected student from Group $B$ obtained the top grade is $0.30.$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262444 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262445 13194139533869 true] [17592186262444 1089 "q-122266" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262444 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533869 true] [17592186262444 1100 "John collects a basket of apples, only $80%$ of them are ripe. The probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6.$  The probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262444 1113 #uuid "9ccfae6b-552a-5354-9916-82dda86bb673" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262444 1145 "Let $W$ be the event that the chosen apple has a worm inside, and let $R$ be the event that the chosen apple is ripe.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(W)=P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $80%$ of the apples are ripe and $20$ are unripe, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.8,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6,$ and the probability that an unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W|R)=0.6,\\,P(W|{R}^{′})=0.1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.6\\cdot 0.8+0.1\\cdot 0.2 \\\\  & =0.48+0.02 \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262446 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262447 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262448 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262449 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262446 13194139533869 true] [17592186262445 1110 #uuid "896d6b93-97e3-54ff-8aa6-dc7bde0cb061" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262446 1034 "0.5" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262446 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262446 1111 #uuid "a2dff9bc-3cad-5e99-8e3c-1556da03de78" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262447 1034 "0.35" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262447 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262447 1111 #uuid "4af3279d-1c2c-50e6-9bc7-7f1ef53fda04" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262448 1034 "0.65" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262448 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262448 1111 #uuid "ac19803a-1c42-59c8-9fac-cc047f52e61e" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262449 1034 "0.45" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262449 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262449 1111 #uuid "73991856-2529-5884-90f1-e4ebe27f7215" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262450 1034 "0.55" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262450 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262450 1111 #uuid "5465c0c1-4e86-5642-8fe1-ddc0b0db7ab1" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262451 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262452 13194139533869 true] [17592186262451 1089 "q-122291" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262451 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533869 true] [17592186262451 1100 "A house building company has undertaken a construction job. It is known that there is a probability of $0.55$ that there will be a strike. If there won't be a strike the probability of completing the job on time is $0.8,$ if there will be a strike, the probability is $0.3.$ What is the probability that the construction job will be completed on time?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262451 1113 #uuid "27d3ad7e-d48b-54a5-8d61-1de17b23f16e" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262451 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that the construction job will be completed on time, and let $S$ be the event that there will be a strike.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the construction job will be completed on time, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|S)P(S)+P(C|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since the probability that there will be a strike is $0.55$ and the probability that there will not be a strike is $0.45,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(S)=0.55,\\,P({S}^{′})=0.45.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that the construction job will be completed on time if there is a strike is $0.3,$ and the probability that the construction job will be completed on time if there is no strike is $0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(C|S)=0.3,\\,P(C|{S}^{′})=0.8.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability  that the construction job will be completed on time is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|S)P(S)+P(C|{S}^{′})P({S}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.3\\cdot 0.55+0.8\\cdot 0.45 \\\\  & =0.165+0.36 \\\\  & =0.525.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262454 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262455 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262456 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262457 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262455 13194139533869 true] [17592186262452 1110 #uuid "47ac19c1-9295-5101-880c-8e3c4e784657" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262453 1034 "0.325" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262453 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262453 1111 #uuid "1a387745-e9d1-5dc2-8f6b-d9820a5548cf" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262454 1034 "0.725" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262454 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262454 1111 #uuid "a7e69c97-762a-5077-b3e5-b09d30c41110" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262455 1034 "0.525" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262455 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262455 1111 #uuid "3e6ef08c-9122-59e3-9cdc-0c9d0110daca" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262456 1034 "0.375" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262456 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262456 1111 #uuid "2b54a060-5fc4-5581-8c01-9707a57fcefe" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262457 1034 "0.555" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262457 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262457 1111 #uuid "0bd0d403-d700-51b3-93c7-cdc7958607d8" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262458 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262459 13194139533869 true] [17592186262458 1089 "q-122293" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262458 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533869 true] [17592186262458 1100 "Two machines ${M}_{1}$ and ${M}_{2}$ in a certain factory produce $75%$ and $25%,$ respectively, of the total daily output of electronic tubes. It is known that $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ and that $5%$ of those produced by ${M}_{2}$ are defective. What is the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262458 1113 #uuid "abb80d0d-3cb2-5dfa-b275-cc5f2ea2f90e" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262458 1145 "Let $D$ be the event that the picked electronic tube is defective, and let $A$ be the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{1}.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the electronic tube is produced by machine ${M}_{2}.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(D)=P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since machine ${M}_{1}$ produces $75%$ of the total daily output of electronic tubes and ${M}_{2}$ the remaining $25%,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.75,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.25.\n$$\n\n- Since $4%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{1}$ are defective, and $5%$ of the tubes produced by machine ${M}_{2}$ are defective, we have\n\n$$\nP(D|A)=0.04,\\,P(D|{A}^{′})=0.05.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly picked tube from a day's production is defective is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(D) & =P(D|A)P(A)+P(D|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.04)(0.75)+(0.05)(0.25) \\\\  & =0.0425.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262460 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262461 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262462 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262463 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262464 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262461 13194139533869 true] [17592186262459 1110 #uuid "c1fc0c6f-c75f-5802-b5b7-f0aec7c451f7" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262460 1034 "0.125" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262460 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262460 1111 #uuid "3a05e34a-131b-50b5-9ec4-4d702741ba54" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262461 1034 "0.0425" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262461 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262461 1111 #uuid "f55530a0-ca6d-5c27-82ae-7e8e7b8e454a" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262462 1034 "0.25" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262462 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262462 1111 #uuid "18c12ccd-0796-5f80-a987-4dd885edf5dc" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262463 1034 "0.0325" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262463 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262463 1111 #uuid "ab1dfe68-966a-566d-85ef-a113794b46f9" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262464 1034 "0.025" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262464 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262464 1111 #uuid "4e9b3790-5a30-5799-aca0-cef5439e20a6" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262465 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262466 13194139533869 true] [17592186262465 1089 "q-122299" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262465 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533869 true] [17592186262465 1100 "Ann has two shuffled $52$-card decks of playing cards, $A$ and $B.$ $2$ red cards of deck $B$ were lost. The cards of the two decks are mixed together. What is the probability that a randomly selected card from the resulting set of cards is red?\n\n*Hint: A standard $52$-card deck contains $26$ red cards and $26$ black cards.*" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262465 1113 #uuid "c445f49f-fb39-57ae-9a43-7a5f707f00c8" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262465 1145 "Let $R$ be the event that the selected card is red, and let $A$ be the event that the card was one of the cards of deck $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the card was one of the cards of deck $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected card is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(R\\cap A),$ we take the number of red cards in deck $A$ $(26)$ and divide by the total number of cards in both decks $(52+50=102).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap A)=\\frac{26}{102}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(R\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of red cards in deck $B$ $(24)$ and divide by the total number of cards in both decks $(102).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(R\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{24}{102}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected card from either deck is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R\\cap A)+P(R\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{26}{102}+\\frac{24}{102} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{51}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262467 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262468 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262469 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262470 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262471 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262471 13194139533869 true] [17592186262466 1110 #uuid "e094f5a2-229b-5ee0-a5c7-817c4582b29f" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262467 1034 "\\frac{4}{51}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262467 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262467 1111 #uuid "285190e3-8c8c-5cd6-9f6c-83719d2c35da" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262468 1034 "\\frac{14}{51}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262468 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262468 1111 #uuid "547cc467-564b-5b2f-a9f5-c395df6c836e" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262469 1034 "\\frac{1}{2}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262469 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262469 1111 #uuid "834602d8-dd0b-561e-be86-aa5dc467b012" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262470 1034 "\\frac{1}{102}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262470 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262470 1111 #uuid "37db604f-5fd2-5139-8e8e-fe23cddac683" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262471 1034 "\\frac{25}{51}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262471 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262471 1111 #uuid "d2d290e9-2f77-525a-bf97-0827cbe5e6d7" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262472 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262473 13194139533869 true] [17592186262472 1089 "q-122396" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262472 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533869 true] [17592186262472 1100 "A medical research center is conducting experiments to examine the relationship between cigarette smoking and cancer in a particular city. The probability that a randomly selected person is a smoker that develops cancer is $0.4,$ while the probability that it is a nonsmoker that develops cancer is $0.25.$ What is the probability that a randomly selected individual from the city develops cancer?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262472 1113 #uuid "65fbcb60-cf4e-5ecd-b1d8-79e08d0131db" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262472 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that an individual develops cancer and let $A$ be the event that individual smokes. Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that an individual does not smoke.\n\nTo calculate the probability that the randomly selected individual develops the cancer, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C\\cap A)+P(C\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nWe  have \n\n$$\nP(C\\cap A)=0.4,\\,P(C\\cap {A}^{′})=0.25\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected  individual  from the city develops cancer is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C\\cap A)+P(C\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =0.4+0.25 \\\\  & =0.65.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262474 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262475 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262476 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262477 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262478 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262477 13194139533869 true] [17592186262473 1110 #uuid "67e2a31e-f187-5620-8f71-7749537bd84d" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262474 1034 "0.6" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262474 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262474 1111 #uuid "3dab1dfc-b4c0-572e-8e5e-4a4c10efb5d1" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262475 1034 "0.4" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262475 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262475 1111 #uuid "b47b1aee-0c2e-5a7b-86ec-204b4f037bca" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262476 1034 "0.25" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262476 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262476 1111 #uuid "17bf85d2-d012-5408-8c1f-dadec9eb2e21" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262477 1034 "0.65" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262477 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262477 1111 #uuid "6e968ce1-8677-5ee4-adb0-490fb47c9ba8" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262478 1034 "0.75" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262478 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262478 1111 #uuid "c3d4455e-26e2-590b-a348-cf272cafd54a" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262479 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262480 13194139533869 true] [17592186262479 1089 "q-122405" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262479 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533869 true] [17592186262479 1100 "John collects a basket of apples, but only $80%$ of them are ripe. The probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside it is $0.6.$  The probability that a randomly selected apple has a worm inside it is $0.5.$ What is the probability of a randomly selected unripe apple having a worm inside it?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262479 1113 #uuid "4a132b6f-cc89-532a-89e1-2c7f30052adc" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262479 1145 "Let $W$ be the event that the chosen apple has a worm inside, and let $R$ be the event that the chosen apple is ripe.  \n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(W)=P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $80%$ of the apples are ripe and $20%$ are unripe, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.8,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.2.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a ripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.6$,  we have\n\n$$\nP(W|R)=0.6.\n$$\n\n- Since the probability that a randomly selected apple in John's collection has a worm inside is $0.5,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(W)=0.5.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(W|{R}^{′}),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(W) & =P(W|R)P(R)+P(W|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.5 & =(0.6)(0.8)+P(W|{R}^{′})(0.2) \\\\ 0.5 & =0.48+0.2P(W|{R}^{′}) \\\\ 0.02 & =0.2P(W|{R}^{′}) \\\\ P(W|{R}^{′}) & =0.1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected unripe apple has a worm inside the apple is $0.1.$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262481 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262482 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262483 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262484 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262485 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262481 13194139533869 true] [17592186262480 1110 #uuid "4b31402d-c144-5067-a816-f376c5650651" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262481 1034 "0.1" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262481 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262481 1111 #uuid "d28c05ad-8b8e-59a9-a245-a897da2ea651" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262482 1034 "0.2" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262482 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262482 1111 #uuid "6a1eb6dd-d98a-56a5-a528-407717fb370a" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262483 1034 "0.13" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262483 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262483 1111 #uuid "ab403a0f-1c5e-5379-825b-efd3faa747c0" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262484 1034 "0.15" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262484 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262484 1111 #uuid "7fd9e616-412a-52e2-ae68-fd193240878b" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262485 1034 "0.12" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262485 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262485 1111 #uuid "42c8a18f-124b-513b-b630-06a5e0912eeb" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262486 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262487 13194139533869 true] [17592186262486 1089 "q-88916" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262486 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533869 true] [17592186262486 1100 "William has a desk with two drawers, $A$ and $B.$ In drawer $A$ he has $14$ markers, of which $3$ are blue. In drawer $B$ he has $21$ markers, of which $4$ are blue. What is the probability that a randomly selected marker from either drawer is blue?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262486 1113 #uuid "122759e3-2f34-5855-9414-74d10d38758c" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262486 1145 "Let $U$ be the event that the chosen marker is blue, and let $A$ be the event that the marker was selected from drawer $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the marker was selected from drawer $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marker is blue, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(U)=P(U\\cap A)+P(U\\cap {A}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(U\\cap A),$ we take the number of blue markers in drawer $A$ $(3)$ and divide by the total number of markers in both drawers $(14+21=35).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(U\\cap A)=\\frac{3}{35}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(U\\cap {A}^{′}),$ we take the number of blue markers on drawer $B$ $(4)$ and divide by the total number of markers in both drawers $(35).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(U\\cap {A}^{′})=\\frac{4}{35}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marker from the desk drawers is blue is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(U) & =P(U\\cap A)+P(U\\cap {A}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{35}+\\frac{4}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{35} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262488 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262489 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262490 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262491 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262492 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262490 13194139533869 true] [17592186262487 1110 #uuid "37c118bc-25fb-527f-8d09-2226a28ecd1a" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262488 1034 "\\frac{2}{7}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262488 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262488 1111 #uuid "dc43f80b-b158-58c4-a2e9-559d5b0115ae" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262489 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262489 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262489 1111 #uuid "832589f0-03d9-588e-8a23-4a846274c098" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262490 1034 "\\frac{1}{5}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262490 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262490 1111 #uuid "1052fac3-7be4-52fd-b8ec-a3c8a43fd226" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262491 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262491 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262491 1111 #uuid "939098bb-7fec-54d4-ac4f-99342bdd68f4" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262492 1034 "\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262492 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262492 1111 #uuid "1a16aa56-bbe5-5f20-867a-26d774905b8d" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262493 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262494 13194139533869 true] [17592186262493 1089 "q-88918" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262493 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139533869 true] [17592186262493 1100 "A pet center registered $34$ dogs and $29$ cats on a particular day. Of the dogs registered that day, $12$ were vaccinated. Of the cats registered that day, $15$ were vaccinated. What is the probability that a randomly selected pet registered that day was vaccinated?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262493 1113 #uuid "9db4b0ac-3bb0-5844-ad10-5311f4573d6a" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262493 1145 "Let $V$ be the event that the chosen pet was vaccinated, and let $D$ be the event that the pet is a dog. Then ${D}^{′}$ is the event that the pet is a cat.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(V)=P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{′})\n$$\n\nTo calculate $P(V\\cap D),$ we take the number of dogs who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(12)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(34+29=63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap D)=\\frac{12}{63}.\n$$\n\nSimilarly, to calculate $P(V\\cap {D}^{′}),$ we take the number of cats who visited the pet center and were vaccinated $(15)$ and divide by the total number of pets $(63).$ So, we have\n\n$$\nP(V\\cap {D}^{′})=\\frac{15}{63}.\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected pet was vaccinated is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(V) & =P(V\\cap D)+P(V\\cap {D}^{′}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12}{63}+\\frac{15}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{27}{63} \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262495 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262496 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262497 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262498 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262499 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262499 13194139533869 true] [17592186262494 1110 #uuid "382551a3-dd9e-5b40-baff-e11b9ef7542f" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262495 1034 "\\frac{4}{5}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262495 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262495 1111 #uuid "b475f41a-bb0b-5847-8d34-af0fea8a3238" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262496 1034 "\\frac{2}{7}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262496 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262496 1111 #uuid "b34226e7-213c-503b-bd3d-25acd14c549d" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262497 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262497 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262497 1111 #uuid "8828c272-930b-5713-84d7-7096938a8219" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262498 1034 "\\frac{1}{7}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262498 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262498 1111 #uuid "4906c734-dedd-5ab2-a21c-58d004ccc6bb" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262499 1034 "\\frac{3}{7}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262499 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262499 1111 #uuid "02fd9981-6671-5bd6-b00a-38c15401e241" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262500 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262501 13194139533869 true] [17592186262500 1089 "q-89027" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262500 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533869 true] [17592186262500 1100 "John keeps his marbles in two boxes, box $A$ and box $B.$ Of the marbles in his collection, $\\frac{3}{4}$ of them are in box $A,$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ of them are in box $B.$ If $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, what is the probability that a randomly selected marble in John's collection is red?" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262500 1113 #uuid "84b999b4-0afb-5f2c-8315-6aad5e7dea21" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262500 1145 "Let $R$ be the event that the chosen marble is red, and let $A$ be the event that the marble was selected from box $A.$ Then, ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the marble was selected from box $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected marble is red, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(R)=P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $\\frac{3}{4}$ of the marbles are in box $A$ and $\\frac{1}{4}$ are in box $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=\\frac{3}{4},\\,P({A}^{′})=\\frac{1}{4}.\n$$\n\n- Since $\\frac{1}{3}$ of the marbles in box $A$ are red and $\\frac{3}{5}$ of the marbles in box $B$ are red, we have\n\n$$\nP(R|A)=\\frac{1}{3},\\,P(R|{A}^{′})=\\frac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected marble is red is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(R) & =P(R|A)P(A)+P(R|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{3})(\\frac{3}{4})+(\\frac{3}{5})(\\frac{1}{4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}+\\frac{3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{5+3}{20} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262502 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262503 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262504 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262505 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262506 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262503 13194139533869 true] [17592186262501 1110 #uuid "a4c417ae-650f-52f6-9548-5bfea8a105b8" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262502 1034 "\\frac{2}{3}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262502 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262502 1111 #uuid "3bd34d60-fb14-573d-a82c-7025c055dab2" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262503 1034 "\\frac{2}{5}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262503 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262503 1111 #uuid "c3c40aa9-239a-596e-aad9-93783485ff93" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262504 1034 "\\frac{3}{5}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262504 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262504 1111 #uuid "7c857ea2-3fe5-5964-9f06-cff8de46ac70" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262505 1034 "\\frac{3}{4}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262505 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262505 1111 #uuid "3b924976-629f-539d-969d-b7eec1d46b72" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262506 1034 "\\frac{7}{10}" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262506 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262506 1111 #uuid "6d3b64b0-24de-5112-8dc7-917a4dc247bc" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262507 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262508 13194139533869 true] [17592186262507 1089 "q-89040" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262507 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533869 true] [17592186262507 1100 "In a gas station, $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline. If $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, what is the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card? Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." 13194139533869 true] [17592186262507 1113 #uuid "2197a11f-d70a-5147-9379-8301a61c86ef" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262507 1145 "Let $C$ be the event that the chosen person paid with a credit card, and let $R$ be the event that the person bought regular gasoline. Then ${R}^{′}$ is the event that the person bought premium gasoline.\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected person paid with a credit card, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(C)=P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $42%$ of the clients buy regular gasoline while $58%$ buy premium gasoline, we have\n\n$$\nP(R)=0.42,\\,P({R}^{′})=0.58.\n$$\n\n- Since $62%$ of the clients that buy regular gasoline paid with a credit card while $55%$ of the clients that buy premium gasoline paid with a credit card, we have\n\n$$\nP(C|R)=0.62,\\,P(C|{R}^{′})=0.55.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability, the probability that a randomly selected client paid with a credit card is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(C) & =P(C|R)P(R)+P(C|{R}^{′})P({R}^{′}) \\\\  & =(0.62)(0.42)+(0.55)(0.58) \\\\  & ≈0.579\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to three decimal places." 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262509 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262510 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262511 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262512 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262513 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1041 "selection" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1104 #edb/ref 17592186262511 13194139533869 true] [17592186262508 1110 #uuid "0fca717d-24e4-5f62-a0b0-0d9b4fd46b9a" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262509 1034 "0.637" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262509 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262509 1111 #uuid "d51a86ea-a1ee-5927-a912-e07c14a069fd" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262510 1034 "0.623" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262510 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262510 1111 #uuid "c9a5973a-5e62-558f-8a46-ba2e836a14f9" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262511 1034 "0.579" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262511 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262511 1111 #uuid "2d637d35-fbcc-5364-be28-b1f129f91b6e" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262512 1034 "0.518" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262512 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262512 1111 #uuid "fca30c93-7a96-5054-875b-06d2bfa68242" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262513 1034 "0.535" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262513 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533869 true] [17592186262513 1111 #uuid "de2eb88a-59c6-570f-ad7d-e46e17506334" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262514 1017 #edb/ref 17592186262515 13194139533869 true] [17592186262514 1089 "q-89044" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262514 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533869 true] [17592186262514 1100 "Jacob has two email accounts, account $A$ and account $B.$ In account $A$ he receives $40%$ of his emails and he receives the remaining $60%$ in account $B.$ Approximately $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam and $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam. What is the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam? Round your answer to $2$ decimal places." 13194139533869 true] [17592186262514 1113 #uuid "52bfa097-4cee-51bc-97ec-d6074dab330f" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262514 1145 "Let $S$ be the event that the chosen email is spam, and let $A$ be the event that the email selected is from account $A.$ Then ${A}^{′}$ is the event that the email selected is from account $B.$\n\nTo calculate the probability that a randomly selected email is spam, we can use the law of total probability:\n\n$$\nP(S)=P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′})\n$$\n\nLet's go through the problem statement and translate each bit of information into mathematical notation.\n\n- Since $40%$ of the emails are received in account $A$ and the remaining $60%$ in account $B,$ we have\n\n$$\nP(A)=0.40,\\,P({A}^{′})=0.60.\n$$\n\n- Since $30%$ of the emails received in account $A$ are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S|A)=0.30.\n$$\n\n- Since $36%$ of the emails received in both accounts are spam, we have \n\n$$\nP(S)=0.36.\n$$\n\nSubstituting the above information into the law of total probability and solving for $P(S|{A}^{′})$, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(S) & =P(S|A)P(A)+P(S|{A}^{′})P({A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.36 & =(0.30)(0.40)+P(S|{A}^{′})(0.60) \\\\ 0.36 & =0.12+0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) \\\\ 0.6P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.24 \\\\ P(S|{A}^{′}) & =0.40.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected email from account $B$ is spam is $0.40.$" 13194139533869 true] [17592186262515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262516 13194139533869 true] [17592186262515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262517 13194139533869 true] [17592186262515 1000 #edb/ref 17592186262518 13194139533869 true] [17592186262515 1000 #